    dict(id="summary", title="Summary", blocks=[
        ("callout", "key", None, r"1) BCE is $-\log$ of the probability of the correct answer; the label $y$ acts as a switch between $-\log\hat y$ and $-\log(1-\hat y)$. 2) It follows from the maximum likelihood of a Bernoulli model and equals the cross-entropy: $H(y)+\mathrm{KL}(y\,\|\,\hat y)$. 3) Paired with the sigmoid, $\partial\ell/\partial z=\hat y-y$: the factor $\sigma'$ cancels, and the gradient is bounded and does not fade on a confident mistake — unlike MSE. 4) BCE is convex in the logit and in the weights of logistic regression; far from the minimum it grows linearly. 5) Compute it from logits: $\max(z,0)-yz+\log(1+e^{-|z|})$. 6) <code>pos_weight</code> shifts the logit by roughly $\ln w_+$, smoothing makes the optimum finite, and the minimum of BCE is reached at calibrated probabilities."),
    ]),
